{"name":"Fresh Sod","hex":"#91a085","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fresh","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Newly produced or obtained; recent.","(of food) Not dried, frozen, or spoiled."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Recently; just recently; most recently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fresh"},{"term":"sod","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Songoora."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The stratum of the surface of the soil which is filled with the roots of grass, or any portion of that surface; turf; sward.","Turf grown and cut specifically for the establishment of lawns."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sod"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sod","blocks":[{"text":"Sod is the upper layer of turf that is harvested for transplanting. Turf consists of a variable thickness of a soil medium that supports a community of turfgrasses. Sod is typically made with grasses that spread laterally through the soil and form a dense mat. In British and Australian English, sod is more commonly known as turf and the term sod is mainly used in agriculture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Rollrasen-01.jpg/330px-Rollrasen-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sod","wikidata":"Q525728"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Anselmo%2C_Nebraska_sod_house.JPG/500px-Anselmo%2C_Nebraska_sod_house.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Anselmo%2C_Nebraska_sod_house.JPG/1280px-Anselmo%2C_Nebraska_sod_house.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Anselmo%2C_Nebraska_sod_house.JPG/500px-Anselmo%2C_Nebraska_sod_house.JPG?utm_source=en.w